     :math-composition "(define (chi-squared observed expected) (let loop ((o observed) (e expected) (acc 0)) (if (null? o) acc (loop (cdr o) (cdr e) (+ acc (/ (square (- (car o) (car e))) (car e)))))))\n(chi-squared '(10 20 30) '(12 18 30))"
     :cs-composition "(define (dist-2d p1 p2) (sqrt (+ (square (- (car p1) (car p2))) (square (- (cadr p1) (cadr p2))))))\n(dist-2d '(0 0) '(3 4))")
    (:base "Chain square with a fold for sum-of-squares." :composition "(define (sos xs) (apply + (map square xs)))
(sos '(3 4 12))" :emergent "map-then-sum." :meta "Belongs to the family of aggregation pipelines. See book-of-composition ch 7.")
    (:eng "Emergent patterns bring known operational discipline (retry, throttle, cache) into the verb chain; the operator inherits the pattern's diagnostics." :math "Composition preserves the algebraic properties of each verb; the emergent structure has its own (usually monoid) invariants worth naming." :cs "The named emergent (cache, throttle, sequencer, adapter, pipeline, projection) is the CS pattern-family label; reach for known-idiom debuggers when it appears.")
))


(verb
  :name "math/sin" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/sin x) -> number"
  :arity "1"
  :contract "x in radians → real in [-1, 1]"
  :summary "Sine of x, where x is in radians."
  :doc "Delegates to Math.sin. The argument is an angle in RADIANS, not degrees — multiply degrees by (/ math/pi 180) first. The result is always in [-1, 1]. Periodic with period 2π. For the inverse, use math/asin."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/sin 0)" :note "Returns 0 — sin(0) = 0.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(math/sin (/ math/pi 2))" :note "Returns 1 — sin(π/2) = 1. Note the argument is in radians.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (deg->rad d) (* d (/ math/pi 180)))\n(display (math/sin (deg->rad 30)))" :note "sin(30°) ≈ 0.5 — convert degrees to radians first.")
  )
  :related ("math/cos" "math/tan" "math/asin" "math/atan2" "math/pi")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The operator needs the vertical component of a force applied at an angle θ (radians)." :program "(define (vertical mag theta) (* mag (math/sin theta)))\n(vertical 10 (/ math/pi 6))" :explanation "Vertical component is magnitude times sin of the angle." :meta "math/sin is correct for the OPPOSITE-over-hypotenuse component. math/cos gives the adjacent (horizontal) component — do not confuse them."
     :eng-program "(define (wave t amp freq) (* amp (math/sin (* 2 math/pi freq t))))\n(wave 0.25 1 1)"
     :math-program "(define (unit-y theta) (math/sin theta))\n(unit-y (/ math/pi 2))")
    (:eng "Log the stated angle units; the most common trig fault is feeding degrees where radians are expected." :math "The audit lens fixes math/sin as a unary periodic function with range [-1,1]; gate on the radians precondition." :cs "Row 2 emphasizes a single pure call; sin may be replaced by its cached value for a fixed argument.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: the opposite-over-hypotenuse projection of an angle."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/sin (/ math/pi 2))")
       (:domain "signal processing (a periodic oscillation)" :code "(define (osc t) (math/sin (* 2 math/pi t)))\n(osc 0.25)")
       (:domain "geometry — the y-coordinate on the unit circle" :code "(define (unit-y theta) (math/sin theta))\n(unit-y 0)"))
     :explanation "Each rung reads the vertical projection of an angle onto the unit circle."
     :meta "The shape transfers because sine is THE canonical periodic projection.")
    (:eng "Portable mental model: sine is always 'height on the circle', across every substrate." :math "Cross-domain sharing is the universal property of the circle group acting on the plane." :cs "The shared shape becomes a reusable oscillator primitive across implementations."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "sin²(x) + cos²(x) = 1 for all x." :program "(< (abs (- (+ (math/square (math/sin 1.2)) (math/square (math/cos 1.2))) 1)) 1e-9)" :invariant "∀ x. sin²x + cos²x = 1" :meta "The Pythagorean identity. If broken, precision drift or a unit bug. See book-of-math ch 11."
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/sin (- math/pi 0.3)) (math/sin 0.3))) 1e-9)"
     :cs-program "(and (<= (math/sin 5) 1) (>= (math/sin 5) -1))")
    (:eng "A runtime failure of the Pythagorean identity is a fault line — the pure algebra must hold." :math "The identity is the defining relation between sine and cosine on the unit circle." :cs "Make the identity a CI property test to catch a regressed trig implementation.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Chain sin with an amplitude and frequency for a wave." :composition "(define (wave t amp freq) (* amp (math/sin (* 2 math/pi freq t))))\n(wave 0.1 2 3)" :emergent "amplitude × sin(2πft) = a sinusoid." :meta "Belongs to the family of oscillator patterns. See book-of-composition ch 14."
     :math-composition "(define (unit-circle theta) (list (math/cos theta) (math/sin theta)))\n(unit-circle (/ math/pi 4))"
     :cs-composition "(define (samples n) (map (lambda (i) (math/sin (* 2 math/pi (/ i n)))) (iota n)))\n(samples 4)")
    (:eng "The oscillator pattern inherits known DSP discipline (windowing, aliasing awareness)." :math "cos + sin compose into a point on the circle; the emergent structure is the circle group." :cs "The named emergent (oscillator / sampler) is a reusable signal primitive.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "math/cos" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/cos x) -> number"
  :arity "1"
  :contract "x in radians → real in [-1, 1]"
  :summary "Cosine of x, where x is in radians."
  :doc "Delegates to Math.cos. The argument is an angle in RADIANS, not degrees. The result is always in [-1, 1]. Cosine is sine shifted by π/2: cos(x) = sin(x + π/2). For the inverse, use math/acos."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/cos 0)" :note "Returns 1 — cos(0) = 1.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(math/cos math/pi)" :note "Returns -1 — cos(π) = -1.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (horizontal mag theta) (* mag (math/cos theta)))\n(display (horizontal 10 (/ math/pi 3)))" :note "Horizontal component of a force at π/3 radians ≈ 5.")
  )
  :related ("math/sin" "math/tan" "math/acos" "math/atan2" "math/pi")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The operator needs the horizontal component of a vector at angle θ (radians)." :program "(define (horizontal mag theta) (* mag (math/cos theta)))\n(horizontal 10 (/ math/pi 3))" :explanation "Horizontal component is magnitude times cosine of the angle." :meta "math/cos gives the ADJACENT-over-hypotenuse component. math/sin gives the vertical — do not swap them."
     :eng-program "(define (power vrms irms phi) (* vrms irms (math/cos phi)))\n(power 120 2 0.2)"
     :math-program "(define (unit-x theta) (math/cos theta))\n(unit-x 0)")
    (:eng "Log the angle units; degrees-for-radians is the classic trig fault." :math "The audit lens fixes math/cos as a unary periodic function, range [-1,1]; gate on radians." :cs "A single pure call; cos may be replaced by its cached value for a fixed argument.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: the adjacent-over-hypotenuse projection of an angle."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/cos math/pi)")
       (:domain "electrical engineering (the power factor)" :code "(define (power-factor phi) (math/cos phi))\n(power-factor 0.2)")
       (:domain "geometry — the x-coordinate on the unit circle" :code "(define (unit-x theta) (math/cos theta))\n(unit-x 0)"))
     :explanation "Each rung reads the horizontal projection of an angle onto the unit circle."
     :meta "The shape transfers because cosine is the canonical horizontal projection.")
    (:eng "Portable model: cosine is 'width on the circle' everywhere." :math "The universal property of the circle group's action on the plane." :cs "A reusable projection primitive across implementations."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "cos(-x) = cos(x) — cosine is even." :program "(< (abs (- (math/cos -1.3) (math/cos 1.3))) 1e-9)" :invariant "∀ x. cos(-x) = cos(x)" :meta "Cosine is an even function. If broken, a sign bug. See book-of-math ch 11."
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (+ (math/square (math/sin 0.7)) (math/square (math/cos 0.7))) 1)) 1e-9)"
     :cs-program "(and (<= (math/cos 5) 1) (>= (math/cos 5) -1))")
    (:eng "A runtime failure of evenness is a fault line — the algebra must hold." :math "Evenness is a defining symmetry of cosine about the y-axis." :cs "Make evenness and the Pythagorean identity CI property tests.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Pair cos with sin to place a point on the unit circle." :composition "(define (on-circle theta r) (list (* r (math/cos theta)) (* r (math/sin theta))))\n(on-circle (/ math/pi 6) 2)" :emergent "r·cos, r·sin = polar-to-cartesian." :meta "Belongs to the family of coordinate-conversion patterns. See book-of-composition ch 12."
     :math-composition "(define (rotate x y theta) (list (- (* x (math/cos theta)) (* y (math/sin theta))) (+ (* x (math/sin theta)) (* y (math/cos theta)))))\n(rotate 1 0 (/ math/pi 2))"
     :cs-composition "(define (ring n r) (map (lambda (i) (let ((a (* 2 math/pi (/ i n)))) (list (* r (math/cos a)) (* r (math/sin a))))) (iota n)))\n(ring 4 1)")
    (:eng "The polar-to-cartesian pattern inherits geometry-pipeline discipline." :math "cos + sin compose into rotations; the emergent structure is SO(2)." :cs "The named emergent (rotation / coordinate transform) is a reusable geometry primitive.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "math/tan" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/tan x) -> number"
  :arity "1"
  :contract "x in radians → real; unbounded near odd multiples of π/2"
  :summary "Tangent of x, where x is in radians."
  :doc "Delegates to Math.tan. The argument is in RADIANS. tan(x) = sin(x)/cos(x), so it is UNBOUNDED and blows up near odd multiples of π/2 (where cos → 0) — expect very large magnitudes, not an error, near those points. For the inverse, use math/atan or math/atan2."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/tan 0)" :note "Returns 0 — tan(0) = 0.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(math/tan (/ math/pi 4))" :note "Returns ≈ 1 — tan(π/4) = 1.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (slope-from-angle theta) (math/tan theta))\n(display (slope-from-angle (/ math/pi 3)))" :note "tan(60°) ≈ 1.732 — the slope of a line at that angle.")
  )
  :related ("math/sin" "math/cos" "math/atan" "math/atan2")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The operator needs the slope (rise/run) of a line inclined at angle θ (radians)." :program "(define (slope theta) (math/tan theta))\n(slope (/ math/pi 4))" :explanation "Slope equals the tangent of the inclination angle." :meta "math/tan is correct for slope-from-angle. Beware θ near π/2 where the slope is unbounded; guard the input if it can approach vertical."
     :eng-program "(define (rise run theta) (* run (math/tan theta)))\n(rise 10 (/ math/pi 6))"
     :math-program "(define (tan-def theta) (/ (math/sin theta) (math/cos theta)))\n(tan-def (/ math/pi 4))")
    (:eng "Log the angle and guard near π/2; the failure mode is a huge value, not an exception." :math "The audit lens fixes math/tan as a unary function with poles at odd multiples of π/2." :cs "A single pure call, but not total in the practical sense near the poles — document the guard.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: the ratio of opposite to adjacent (rise over run)."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/tan (/ math/pi 4))")
       (:domain "surveying (a grade/slope)" :code "(define (grade theta) (math/tan theta))\n(grade (/ math/pi 12))")
       (:domain "geometry — sin over cos" :code "(define (tan-def theta) (/ (math/sin theta) (math/cos theta)))\n(tan-def 0)"))
     :explanation "Each rung reads a slope as the opposite/adjacent ratio."
     :meta "The shape transfers because tangent IS the slope of the angle.")
    (:eng "Portable model: tangent is 'slope from angle' everywhere." :math "The ratio sin/cos on the unit circle." :cs "A reusable slope primitive across implementations."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "tan(x) = sin(x)/cos(x) away from the poles." :program "(< (abs (- (math/tan 0.6) (/ (math/sin 0.6) (math/cos 0.6)))) 1e-9)" :invariant "∀ x with cos(x) ≠ 0. tan x = sin x / cos x" :meta "The defining ratio. If broken, an implementation bug. See book-of-math ch 11."
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/tan (- 0.5)) (- (math/tan 0.5)))) 1e-9)"
     :cs-program "(< (abs (- (math/tan (/ math/pi 4)) 1)) 1e-9)")
    (:eng "A failure of the sin/cos identity is a fault line away from the poles." :math "The ratio identity is the definition of tangent." :cs "Property-test the identity on inputs bounded away from π/2.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Use tan to turn an angle into a slope for a line." :composition "(define (line-at theta) (lambda (x) (* x (math/tan theta))))\n((line-at (/ math/pi 4)) 5)" :emergent "closure over tan(θ) = a line through the origin at that angle." :meta "Belongs to the family of parametric-line patterns. See book-of-composition ch 12."
     :math-composition "(define (project dist theta) (list (* dist (math/cos theta)) (* dist (math/sin theta))))\n(project 10 (math/atan 1))"
     :cs-composition "(define (slopes angles) (map math/tan angles))\n(slopes (list 0 (/ math/pi 6) (/ math/pi 4)))")
    (:eng "The parametric-line pattern inherits geometry-pipeline discipline." :math "tan composes with atan into the identity away from poles; the emergent structure is a chart of the circle." :cs "The named emergent (line factory / slope map) is a reusable geometry primitive.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "math/asin" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/asin x) -> number"
  :arity "1"
  :contract "x in [-1, 1] → radians in [-π/2, π/2]; |x| > 1 → NaN"
  :summary "Arcsine (inverse sine) of x; returns an angle in radians."
  :doc "Delegates to Math.asin. The argument MUST be in [-1, 1] — outside that range the result is NaN (not an error), because sine never exceeds 1 in magnitude. The result is the principal angle in [-π/2, π/2], in radians. Inverse of math/sin on that principal branch."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/asin 0)" :note "Returns 0 — asin(0) = 0.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(math/asin 1)" :note "Returns ≈ 1.5708 (π/2) — the largest principal arcsine.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (angle-from-ratio opp hyp) (math/asin (/ opp hyp)))\n(display (angle-from-ratio 1 2))" :note "asin(0.5) ≈ 0.5236 (π/6) — recover an angle from an opposite/hypotenuse ratio.")
  )
  :related ("math/sin" "math/acos" "math/atan" "math/atan2")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The operator has an opposite/hypotenuse ratio and needs the angle (radians)." :program "(define (angle opp hyp) (math/asin (/ opp hyp)))\n(angle 1 2)" :explanation "Arcsine inverts the opposite-over-hypotenuse ratio into an angle." :meta "math/asin is correct when you have the SINE ratio. If you have opposite AND adjacent, prefer math/atan2 — it covers all four quadrants; asin only spans [-π/2, π/2]."
     :eng-program "(define (incline rise hyp) (math/asin (/ rise hyp)))\n(incline 3 5)"
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/sin (math/asin 0.4)) 0.4)) 1e-9)")
    (:eng "Guard the domain: a ratio > 1 (e.g. from a measurement error) yields NaN, not an exception." :math "The audit lens fixes math/asin as the principal inverse of sine, domain [-1,1]." :cs "A single pure call; the NaN-on-out-of-domain contract must be stated so callers check.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: recover an angle from a bounded sine ratio."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/asin 0.5)")
       (:domain "navigation (a bearing from a sine)" :code "(define (elevation ratio) (math/asin ratio))\n(elevation 0.5)")
       (:domain "geometry — inverse of sin on [-π/2, π/2]" :code "(< (abs (- (math/asin (math/sin 0.3)) 0.3)) 1e-9)"))
     :explanation "Each rung inverts a bounded sine ratio back into a principal angle."
     :meta "The shape transfers because arcsine is the principal inverse of sine.")
    (:eng "Portable model: asin is 'angle from a sine ratio', within the principal branch." :math "The principal-branch inverse of the sine restriction." :cs "A reusable angle-recovery primitive."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "sin(asin(x)) = x for x in [-1, 1]." :program "(< (abs (- (math/sin (math/asin 0.6)) 0.6)) 1e-9)" :invariant "∀ x ∈ [-1,1]. sin(asin x) = x" :meta "Right inverse of sine on the domain. If broken, a branch or precision bug. See book-of-math ch 11."
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/asin 1) (/ math/pi 2))) 1e-9)"
     :cs-program "(and (<= (math/asin 1) (/ math/pi 2)) (>= (math/asin -1) (- (/ math/pi 2))))")
    (:eng "A failure of sin(asin x)=x is a fault line on the valid domain." :math "The right-inverse identity defines the principal branch." :cs "Property-test on random x in [-1,1]; also assert NaN for |x|>1.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Use asin to recover an incline angle, then feed it forward." :composition "(define (incline-and-run rise hyp) (let ((theta (math/asin (/ rise hyp)))) (list theta (math/cos theta))))\n(incline-and-run 3 5)" :emergent "asin then cos = recover the angle, then its adjacent component." :meta "Belongs to the family of recover-then-reproject patterns. See book-of-composition ch 12."
     :math-composition "(define (both-angles ratio) (list (math/asin ratio) (math/acos ratio)))\n(both-angles 0.5)"
     :cs-composition "(define (angles ratios) (map math/asin ratios))\n(angles (list 0 0.5 1))")
    (:eng "The recover-then-reproject pattern inherits geometry-pipeline discipline." :math "asin composes with sin into the identity on [-1,1]; the emergent structure is a chart." :cs "The named emergent (angle recovery map) is a reusable geometry primitive.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "math/acos" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/acos x) -> number"
  :arity "1"
  :contract "x in [-1, 1] → radians in [0, π]; |x| > 1 → NaN"
  :summary "Arccosine (inverse cosine) of x; returns an angle in radians."
  :doc "Delegates to Math.acos. The argument MUST be in [-1, 1] — outside that range the result is NaN. The result is the principal angle in [0, π], in radians. Inverse of math/cos on that principal branch. Commonly used to recover the angle between two vectors from their normalized dot product."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/acos 1)" :note "Returns 0 — acos(1) = 0.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(math/acos 0)" :note "Returns ≈ 1.5708 (π/2).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (angle-between dot) (math/acos dot))\n(display (angle-between 0.5))" :note "For unit vectors, acos of their dot product ≈ 1.0472 (π/3) is the angle between them.")
  )
  :related ("math/cos" "math/asin" "math/atan" "math/atan2")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The operator has the normalized dot product of two unit vectors and needs the angle between them (radians)." :program "(define (angle-between dot) (math/acos dot))\n(angle-between 0.5)" :explanation "Arccosine of the dot product of unit vectors gives the angle between them." :meta "math/acos is correct for the vector-angle from a dot product. Ensure the dot product is CLAMPED to [-1,1] first — floating-point drift can push it just outside and produce NaN."
     :eng-program "(define (bond-angle dot) (math/acos dot))\n(bond-angle -0.333)"
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/cos (math/acos 0.4)) 0.4)) 1e-9)")
    (:eng "Clamp the input to [-1,1] before calling; drift-induced NaN is the classic acos fault." :math "The audit lens fixes math/acos as the principal inverse of cosine, domain [-1,1], range [0,π]." :cs "A single pure call; state the NaN-on-out-of-domain contract so callers clamp.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: recover an angle from a bounded cosine ratio."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/acos 0)")
       (:domain "3D graphics (angle between vectors from a dot product)" :code "(define (vec-angle dot) (math/acos dot))\n(vec-angle 0.5)")
       (:domain "geometry — inverse of cos on [0, π]" :code "(< (abs (- (math/acos (math/cos 1.0)) 1.0)) 1e-9)"))
     :explanation "Each rung inverts a bounded cosine ratio back into a principal angle in [0, π]."
     :meta "The shape transfers because arccosine is the principal inverse of cosine.")
    (:eng "Portable model: acos is 'angle from a cosine / dot product', within [0, π]." :math "The principal-branch inverse of the cosine restriction." :cs "A reusable vector-angle primitive."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "cos(acos(x)) = x for x in [-1, 1]." :program "(< (abs (- (math/cos (math/acos -0.7)) -0.7)) 1e-9)" :invariant "∀ x ∈ [-1,1]. cos(acos x) = x" :meta "Right inverse of cosine on the domain. If broken, a branch or precision bug. See book-of-math ch 11."
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (+ (math/asin 0.5) (math/acos 0.5)) (/ math/pi 2))) 1e-9)"
     :cs-program "(and (<= (math/acos -1) math/pi) (>= (math/acos 1) 0))")
    (:eng "A failure of cos(acos x)=x is a fault line on the valid domain." :math "The complementary identity asin(x)+acos(x)=π/2 also holds and is worth a check." :cs "Property-test on random x in [-1,1]; also assert NaN for |x|>1.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Use acos on a clamped dot product for a robust vector angle." :composition "(define (clamp x lo hi) (max lo (min hi x)))\n(define (safe-angle dot) (math/acos (clamp dot -1 1)))\n(safe-angle 1.0000001)" :emergent "clamp then acos = a drift-safe vector-angle." :meta "Belongs to the family of guard-then-compute patterns. See book-of-composition ch 10."
     :math-composition "(define (both ratio) (list (math/acos ratio) (- (/ math/pi 2) (math/asin ratio))))\n(both 0.3)"
     :cs-composition "(define (angles dots) (map (lambda (d) (math/acos (max -1 (min 1 d)))) dots))\n(angles (list -1 0 1))")
    (:eng "The guard-then-compute pattern inherits numerical-robustness discipline." :math "acos composes with cos into the identity on [-1,1]; asin+acos is constant π/2." :cs "The named emergent (clamped angle map) is a reusable numerically-safe primitive.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "math/atan" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/atan x) -> number"
  :arity "1"
  :contract "any real x → radians in (-π/2, π/2)"
  :summary "Arctangent (inverse tangent) of x; returns an angle in radians."
  :doc "Delegates to Math.atan. Accepts ANY real number (no domain restriction) and returns the principal angle in (-π/2, π/2), in radians. Because it takes a single slope value it CANNOT distinguish quadrants — use math/atan2 when you have separate y and x components and need the full (-π, π] range."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/atan 0)" :note "Returns 0 — atan(0) = 0.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(math/atan 1)" :note "Returns ≈ 0.7854 (π/4) — atan(1) = π/4.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (angle-of-slope m) (math/atan m))\n(display (angle-of-slope 1.732))" :note "atan(√3) ≈ 1.0472 (π/3) — recover the inclination angle from a slope.")
  )
  :related ("math/tan" "math/atan2" "math/asin" "math/acos")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The operator has a slope (rise/run) and needs the inclination angle (radians)." :program "(define (incline slope) (math/atan slope))\n(incline 1)" :explanation "Arctangent of the slope gives the inclination angle." :meta "math/atan is correct for a single slope value. If you have SEPARATE rise and run (and the run may be negative or zero), use math/atan2 — atan alone loses the quadrant and cannot represent vertical."
     :eng-program "(define (pitch rise run) (math/atan (/ rise run)))\n(pitch 3 4)"
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/tan (math/atan 2)) 2)) 1e-9)")
    (:eng "If run can be zero or negative, atan silently gives the wrong quadrant — prefer atan2 there." :math "The audit lens fixes math/atan as a total unary function with range (-π/2, π/2)." :cs "A single pure call; note the quadrant limitation in the caveats.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: recover an angle from an unbounded slope value."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/atan 1)")
       (:domain "civil engineering (a roof pitch from rise/run)" :code "(define (pitch slope) (math/atan slope))\n(pitch 0.5)")
       (:domain "geometry — inverse of tan on (-π/2, π/2)" :code "(< (abs (- (math/atan (math/tan 0.5)) 0.5)) 1e-9)"))
     :explanation "Each rung inverts an unbounded slope back into a principal angle."
     :meta "The shape transfers because arctangent is the principal inverse of tangent.")
    (:eng "Portable model: atan is 'angle from a single slope', within (-π/2, π/2)." :math "The principal-branch inverse of the tangent restriction." :cs "A reusable angle-from-slope primitive."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "tan(atan(x)) = x for all real x." :program "(< (abs (- (math/tan (math/atan 3.5)) 3.5)) 1e-9)" :invariant "∀ x. tan(atan x) = x" :meta "Right inverse of tangent on the whole real line. If broken, a branch bug. See book-of-math ch 11."
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/atan -2) (- (math/atan 2)))) 1e-9)"
     :cs-program "(and (< (math/atan 1e9) (/ math/pi 2)) (> (math/atan -1e9) (- (/ math/pi 2))))")
    (:eng "A failure of tan(atan x)=x is a fault line for all x." :math "atan is an odd function; atan(-x) = -atan(x) is a second checkable identity." :cs "Property-test on random reals, including large magnitudes near the asymptotes.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Use atan to turn a slope into an angle, then a direction." :composition "(define (heading slope) (let ((a (math/atan slope))) (list a (math/cos a) (math/sin a))))\n(heading 1)" :emergent "atan then cos/sin = slope to a unit direction vector." :meta "Belongs to the family of slope-to-direction patterns. See book-of-composition ch 12."
     :math-composition "(define (approx-pi) (* 4 (math/atan 1)))\n(approx-pi)"
     :cs-composition "(define (angles slopes) (map math/atan slopes))\n(angles (list 0 1 -1 100))")
    (:eng "The slope-to-direction pattern inherits geometry-pipeline discipline." :math "4·atan(1) = π — a classic identity; the emergent structure links tangent to the circle constant." :cs "The named emergent (direction map) is a reusable geometry primitive.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "math/atan2" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/atan2 y x) -> number"
  :arity "2"
  :contract "any real y, x → radians in (-π, π]; note argument order is (y x)"
  :summary "Two-argument arctangent of y/x that respects the quadrant; returns an angle in radians."
  :doc "Delegates to Math.atan2. Takes SEPARATE y and x components — NOTE the order is (math/atan2 y x), y FIRST — and returns the angle of the point (x, y) from the positive x-axis, in (-π, π] radians. Unlike math/atan it uses the signs of BOTH arguments to pick the correct quadrant, and it handles x = 0 (vertical) correctly. This is the verb to convert cartesian coordinates to a polar angle."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/atan2 1 1)" :note "Returns ≈ 0.7854 (π/4) — the point (1,1) is at 45°.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(math/atan2 0 -1)" :note "Returns ≈ 3.1416 (π) — the point (-1,0) is at 180°. atan alone could not tell this from 0.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (bearing dx dy) (math/atan2 dy dx))\n(display (bearing -1 -1))" :note "Point (-1,-1) is at ≈ -2.356 rad (-135°) — the full quadrant is preserved, y passed first.")
  )
  :related ("math/atan" "math/sin" "math/cos" "math/hypot")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The operator has a displacement (dx, dy) and needs the heading angle, correct in all four quadrants (radians)." :program "(define (heading dx dy) (math/atan2 dy dx))\n(heading -1 -1)" :explanation "atan2 with y first returns the quadrant-correct angle of the displacement." :meta "math/atan2 is correct here — math/atan (/ dy dx) would collapse (-1,-1) and (1,1) to the same angle and blow up when dx=0. Remember the (y x) order."
     :eng-program "(define (wind-dir north east) (math/atan2 east north))\n(wind-dir -1 0)"
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/atan2 1 1) (/ math/pi 4))) 1e-9)")
    (:eng "The two classic atan2 faults: swapping the (y x) order, and reaching for atan instead — both give wrong headings. Log the component order." :math "The audit lens fixes math/atan2 as a binary function, range (-π, π], quadrant-aware." :cs "A single pure call; the (y, x) argument order is the most error-prone part — state it loudly.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: cartesian (x, y) to a quadrant-correct polar angle."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/atan2 1 1)")
       (:domain "robotics (a heading from a displacement vector)" :code "(define (heading dx dy) (math/atan2 dy dx))\n(heading 0 1)")
       (:domain "geometry — polar angle of a point, all quadrants" :code "(define (polar-angle x y) (math/atan2 y x))\n(polar-angle -1 0)"))
     :explanation "Each rung reads the polar angle of a point using both signs to pick the quadrant."
     :meta "The shape transfers because atan2 is THE cartesian-to-polar angle.")
    (:eng "Portable model: atan2 is 'the true heading of a vector', across every substrate." :math "The full argument function of a nonzero complex number / plane vector." :cs "A reusable cartesian-to-polar primitive."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "atan2(sin θ, cos θ) = θ for θ in (-π, π]." :program "(< (abs (- (math/atan2 (math/sin 1.2) (math/cos 1.2)) 1.2)) 1e-9)" :invariant "∀ θ ∈ (-π,π]. atan2(sin θ, cos θ) = θ" :meta "Round-trips a point on the unit circle back to its angle, quadrant intact. If broken, a quadrant or order bug. See book-of-math ch 11."
     :math-program "(< (abs (- (math/atan2 1 1) (/ math/pi 4))) 1e-9)"
     :cs-program "(and (<= (math/atan2 1 -1) math/pi) (> (math/atan2 -1 -1) (- math/pi)))")
    (:eng "A failure of the unit-circle round-trip is a fault line — usually a swapped (y x)." :math "The round-trip identity distinguishes atan2 from atan by preserving the quadrant." :cs "Property-test θ across all four quadrants; assert the (y, x) order in a doc test.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Pair atan2 with hypot to convert cartesian to full polar coordinates." :composition "(define (to-polar x y) (list (math/hypot x y) (math/atan2 y x)))\n(to-polar 3 4)" :emergent "hypot + atan2 = cartesian-to-polar (r, θ)." :meta "Belongs to the family of coordinate-conversion patterns. See book-of-composition ch 12."
     :math-composition "(define (angle-diff a b) (math/atan2 (math/sin (- a b)) (math/cos (- a b))))\n(angle-diff 3 0.2)"
     :cs-composition "(define (headings points) (map (lambda (p) (math/atan2 (cadr p) (car p))) points))\n(headings (list (list 1 1) (list -1 1) (list -1 -1)))")
    (:eng "The cartesian-to-polar pattern inherits geometry-pipeline discipline; angle-diff via atan2 avoids wraparound bugs." :math "hypot + atan2 compose into the polar chart; the emergent structure is the plane's polar coordinates." :cs "The named emergent (to-polar / normalized angle-difference) is a reusable geometry primitive.")
)
)

;; ─── Motoi-own numeric conveniences (undocumented until 2026-08-02) ────
;; The verbs below are bound in the engine and were not in this chapter.
;; SEVERAL OF THEM SHADOW A STANDARD NAME UNDER A DIVERGENT SPELLING:
;;
;;   ceil     is a second name for R7RS-small `ceiling` (same implementation)
;;   round2   is NOT R7RS-small `round`; it is round-to-2-decimals
;;   =?       is NOT R7RS-small `=`; it is a second name for `equal?`
;;   pi       is a BOUND VALUE, not a procedure -- (pi) raises
;;
;; The conformance ladder (CLAUDE.md, locked 2026-07-24) says a hand-rolled
;; verb duplicating a standard one under a divergent name is a bug. These
;; records document the bindings that exist today and name the standard verb
;; to prefer. Do not read :std "Motoi" as an endorsement of the spelling.
;; Every note below was produced by running the snippet, not from the source.

(verb :name "pi" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "value"
  :signature "pi -> number"
  :summary "The bound VALUE 3.141592653589793 -- not a procedure. Write pi, never (pi): calling it raises \"not a function\"."
  :explanation "R7RS-small has no pi; a portable program writes (* 4 (atan 1)). Motoi binds the name directly to the double closest to the real number pi, so pi evaluates to 3.141592653589793 with no call. This trips people because the engine source comments it as \"(pi) -> 3.14159\", which is wrong: (procedure? pi) is #f and (pi) raises \"not a function: 3.141592653589793\". There is also a math/pi in the math/* teaching layer. pi is irrational, so no exact number names it: the binding is the double closest to it, and it carries the usual consequence -- (sin pi) is 1.2246467991473532e-16, not 0. Any code that tests trigonometric results against 0 exactly will be disappointed; compare within a tolerance."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "pi" :note "3.141592653589793 — a bare name, evaluated, with no parentheses.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(guard (e (#t (error-object-message e))) (pi))" :note "\"not a function: 3.141592653589793\" — writing (pi) raises. pi is a value binding, not a nullary procedure.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (sin pi) (cos pi))" :note "(1.2246467991473532e-16 -1.0) — sin of pi is not 0. pi is the nearest double to pi, not pi, so the sine of it is the tiny residue. Compare trig results within a tolerance, never against 0 with =.")
  )
  :caveats ("It is a VALUE, not a procedure. (pi) raises \"not a function: 3.141592653589793\", and (procedure? pi) is #f. The comment in src/base.js says otherwise and the comment is wrong." "Not R7RS-small. A portable Scheme writes (* 4 (atan 1)); Motoi's pi is a Motoi-own binding." "(sin pi) is 1.2246467991473532e-16, not 0, because pi is a double. This bites every angle-wrapping and zero-crossing test." "math/pi in the math/* teaching layer is a separate binding for the same constant; two names, one number.")
  :related ("math/pi" "math/tau" "atan" "atan2" "sin" "cos")
  :provenance "Motoi-own value binding (src/base.js). Not in R7RS-small. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")

(verb :name "ceil" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(ceil x) -> number"
  :summary "SHADOW SPELLING of R7RS-small `ceiling`, pointing at the identical implementation. Smallest integer not less than x. Prefer `ceiling` in new code."
  :explanation "src/base.js binds ceil and ceiling to the same expression, so they cannot disagree: (list (ceiling 3.2) (ceil 3.2)) is (4 4). R7RS-small 6.2.6 names this operation `ceiling` and knows nothing of `ceil`; the short spelling is C and JavaScript heritage kept for cart authors who met it first. That makes it a flagged divergent-name binding under the conformance ladder, not a distinct verb. Both handle Motoi's exact rationals -- (ceil 7/2) is 4 -- and both inherit the single-number-type consequence: R7RS says (ceiling 3.0) is the INEXACT 3.0, but Motoi has no exactness to preserve and prints 3."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(ceil 3.2)" :note "4.0 — round up to the next integer.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (ceiling 3.2) (ceil 3.2))" :note "(4.0 4.0) — the same implementation under two names. `ceiling` is the R7RS-small one; reach for it.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (ceil -3.2) (ceil 3.0) (ceil 7/2))" :note "(-3.0 3.0 4) — ceiling of a negative goes toward zero; an already-integral 3.0 comes back as 3.0, keeping the argument's inexactness the way R7RS-small asks; and an exact rational 7/2 is handled, answering the exact 4.")
  )
  :caveats ("It is a divergent spelling of the R7RS-small verb `ceiling`, bound to the same implementation. Prefer `ceiling`; `ceil` is not portable to any other Scheme." "No type check. (ceil \"x\") is +nan.0 rather than an error, and it will not raise -- the NaN travels." "(ceil 3.0) is 3.0. R7RS-small requires the result to keep the argument's exactness, and it does; it answered 3 until the exactness tower landed 2026-08-03." "A bare top-level (ceil \"x\") auto-prints NaN while (display (ceil \"x\")) prints +nan.0. The display spelling is the Scheme one.")
  :related ("ceiling" "floor" "round" "truncate" "math/ceil")
  :provenance "Motoi-own alias for R7RS-small 6.2.6 `ceiling` (src/base.js, same expression). Divergent-name binding flagged 2026-08-02.")

(verb :name "sign" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(sign x) -> number"
  :summary "-1 for a negative number, 0 for zero, 1 for a positive one. Motoi-own; R7RS-small has no sign verb."
  :explanation "sign is Math.sign: it collapses a number to its direction and throws away the size. Zero is its own case rather than being folded into either side, so the range is exactly {-1, 0, 1} on real input. It is a favourite in cart code for turning a difference into a step direction -- (sign (- target current)) is -1, 0 or 1 and can be added to a position each tick. Note that -0.0 answers 0, not -1, which is the sensible reading but is worth knowing if you arrived at the zero by negating. There is no type check: (sign \"x\") is +nan.0, and a NaN compared against anything is false, so a downstream (= (sign v) 0) quietly takes the wrong branch."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sign -3)" :note "-1 — negative in, -1 out; the magnitude 3 is discarded.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (sign -3) (sign 0) (sign 4) (sign -0.5))" :note "(-1 0 1 -1) — three outputs only. -0.5 gives -1 exactly as -3 does; size never survives.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (sign (- 10 4)) (sign (- 4 10)) (sign (- 7 7)))" :note "(1 -1 0) — the step-toward idiom: sign of a difference is the direction to move, and 0 means already there.")
  )
  :caveats ("Not R7RS-small. sign is a Motoi-own verb; no standard Scheme has it under this name." "No type check. (sign \"x\") is +nan.0, and NaN compares false against everything, so (= (sign v) 0) can silently take the wrong branch." "(sign -0.0) is 0, not -1. Negative zero has no sign here." "It discards magnitude entirely. If you need both direction and size, keep the original number.")
  :related ("abs" "clamp" "magnitude" "negative?" "positive?" "zero?")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js, Math.sign). Not in R7RS-small. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")

(verb :name "clamp" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(clamp x lo hi) -> number"
  :summary "x confined to the range lo..hi: lo when x is below, hi when x is above, x otherwise. Motoi-own; R7RS-small has no clamp."
  :explanation "clamp is min(hi, max(lo, x)) written once so cart code does not write it twenty times. It is the standard way to keep a sprite on screen, a health bar in 0..100, or an easing parameter in 0..1. The argument ORDER is value-first: (clamp x lo hi), not (clamp lo hi x). Two failure modes are worth knowing because neither raises. If the bounds are given backwards, the expression still evaluates and returns the SECOND bound: (clamp 5 10 0) is 0. And if hi is omitted, min(undefined, ...) is +nan.0: (clamp 5 0) is +nan.0, which then flows into a coordinate or an index and fails somewhere else entirely."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(clamp 15 0 10)" :note "10 — above the top, so the top is returned.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (clamp -5 0 10) (clamp 5 0 10) (clamp 15 0 10))" :note "(0 5 10) — below, inside, above. Only the middle case gives back the argument.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (clamp 5 10 0) (clamp 0.5 0 1))" :note "(0 0.5) — the first call has its bounds reversed and does not complain: it returns 0, the hi argument. clamp cannot tell a swapped range from a real one.")
  )
  :caveats ("Not R7RS-small. clamp is Motoi-own; there is no standard Scheme clamp." "Reversed bounds do NOT raise. (clamp 5 10 0) is 0 -- it silently returns the hi argument. Check your argument order; it is value, lo, hi." "A missing hi gives +nan.0, not an arity error: (clamp 5 0) is +nan.0 and the NaN travels into whatever consumes it." "No type check on any argument.")
  :related ("min" "max" "lerp" "sign" "math/clamp")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js, Math.min/Math.max). Not in R7RS-small. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")

(verb :name "round2" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(round2 x) -> number"
  :summary "x rounded to two decimal places -- the money rounding. NOT a variant of R7RS-small `round`, despite the name: `round` rounds to an integer and rounds halves to EVEN, round2 keeps two decimals and rounds halves UP."
  :explanation "round2 is Math.round(x * 100) / 100. The 2 in the name is the number of decimal places kept, which is why the name misleads: a reader who knows R7RS `round` expects a rounding MODE, not a precision. The two verbs differ twice over. First in target -- (round 19.999) is 20 and so is (round2 19.999), but (round2 3.14159) is 3.14 while (round 3.14159) is 3. Second in tie-breaking -- R7RS-small 6.2.6 requires `round` to send halves to the even integer, while round2 sends them up, which is not symmetric about zero: (round2 2.675) is 2.68 but (round2 -2.675) is -2.67. And round2 does its multiply-round-divide trip in binary floating point rather than over exact rationals, so a decimal that looks like an exact tie is not one: (round2 1.005) is 1, not 1.01, because 1.005 * 100 is 100.49999999999999. Do not use round2 for money you must balance."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(round2 3.14159)" :note "3.14 — two decimal places kept, the rest dropped.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (round2 3.14159) (round2 2.5) (round2 1.005))" :note "(3.14 2.5 1) — 2.5 already has fewer than two decimals so it passes through untouched, and 1.005 rounds DOWN to 1 rather than up to 1.01, because 1.005 * 100 is 100.49999999999999 in binary floating point.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (round2 2.675) (round2 -2.675))" :note "(2.68 -2.67) — not symmetric about zero. Halves go UP, toward positive infinity, so the positive tie gains a cent and the negative one loses one. R7RS-small `round` would send both to an even integer instead.")
  )
  :caveats ("It is NOT R7RS-small `round` under another name. `round` gives an integer with round-half-to-even; round2 gives two decimals with round-half-up. Reaching for round2 when you meant round is a real bug and nothing will raise." "It is not reliable for money. (round2 1.005) is 1, not 1.01, because the decimal is not exactly representable as a double. If cents must balance, work in integer cents." "Rounding is not symmetric about zero: (round2 2.675) is 2.68 and (round2 -2.675) is -2.67." "An integral result prints without a decimal point: (round2 19.999) is 20, not 20.00. There is no fixed-decimal formatting here, only a number." "No type check. (round2 \"x\") is +nan.0.")
  :related ("round" "math/round" "math/round-half-up" "math/round-to-place" "ceil" "floor" "truncate")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js, Math.round(x*100)/100). Not in R7RS-small; divergent-name shadow of `round` flagged 2026-08-02.")

(verb :name "lerp" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(lerp a b t) -> number"
  :summary "Linear interpolation: the point t of the way from a to b, computed as a + (b - a) * t. Motoi-own; R7RS-small has no lerp. t is NOT clamped."
  :explanation "lerp is the workhorse of animation and easing: t = 0 gives a, t = 1 gives b, t = 0.5 gives the midpoint. The arguments are endpoints-first, parameter-last. Two things are worth internalising. First, t is not confined to 0..1 -- (lerp 0 100 2) is 200 and (lerp 0 100 -1) is -100, so lerp EXTRAPOLATES past both ends. That is often what you want for overshoot, and it is a bug when the t came from an unclamped elapsed-time ratio; pair it with clamp when you need containment. Second, it is floating point, so the arithmetic does not always land where the decimal suggests: (lerp 0.1 0.2 0.5) is 0.15000000000000002, not 0.15. Swapping the endpoints reverses the direction rather than erroring: (lerp 100 0 0.25) is 75."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(lerp 0 100 0.25)" :note "25 — a quarter of the way from 0 to 100.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (lerp 0 100 0) (lerp 0 100 0.5) (lerp 0 100 1))" :note "(0 50 100) — the two endpoints are exact at t = 0 and t = 1, and the midpoint sits halfway.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (lerp 0 100 2) (lerp 0 100 -1) (lerp 0.1 0.2 0.5))" :note "(200 -100 0.15000000000000002) — t outside 0..1 extrapolates instead of raising or clamping, and the halfway point between 0.1 and 0.2 is not the 0.15 you wrote. Wrap t in clamp when containment matters.")
  )
  :caveats ("Not R7RS-small. lerp is Motoi-own." "t is NOT clamped. (lerp 0 100 2) is 200. If t is an elapsed-time ratio that can overrun, write (lerp a b (clamp t 0 1))." "Floating point: (lerp 0.1 0.2 0.5) is 0.15000000000000002. Do not compare a lerp result with = against a decimal you typed." "Swapped endpoints do not raise; (lerp 100 0 0.25) is 75, a valid answer to a different question." "No type check on any argument.")
  :related ("clamp" "math/lerp" "sign" "dist")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js, a + (b - a) * t). Not in R7RS-small. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")

(verb :name "dist" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(dist x1 y1 x2 y2) -> number"
  :summary "Euclidean distance between the 2-D points (x1,y1) and (x2,y2). Four separate numbers, not two points. Motoi-own; R7RS-small has no dist."
  :explanation "dist is Math.hypot(x2 - x1, y2 - y1), which is the ordinary straight-line distance and is computed in a way that avoids overflowing on large coordinates. The signature is the thing to remember: FOUR loose numbers in x, y, x, y order -- there is no point object involved. It sits in the collision group with near?, in-rect? and overlap?, and near? is usually the better verb when you are about to compare the result against a radius, because comparing squared distances (or letting near? do it) avoids the square root. Because it is a plain arithmetic wrapper, a missing argument does not raise: (dist 0 0 3) is +nan.0, and a NaN compared against a radius is false, so a collision simply never fires and nothing tells you why."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(dist 0 0 3 4)" :note "5 — the 3-4-5 triangle.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (dist 0 0 3 4) (dist 2 2 2 2) (dist -1 -1 2 3))" :note "(5 0 5) — a point is distance 0 from itself, and negative coordinates are fine; only the differences matter.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(dist 0 0 1 1)" :note "1.4142135623730951 — the diagonal of a unit square is irrational, so you get the nearest double, not an exact root. Never test a distance for equality with =.")
  )
  :caveats ("Not R7RS-small. dist is Motoi-own." "It takes FOUR numbers, not two points: (dist x1 y1 x2 y2). Passing a pair of lists does not raise, it gives +nan.0." "A missing argument gives +nan.0 rather than an arity error: (dist 0 0 3) is +nan.0, and NaN compares false against every radius, so the collision silently never fires." "The result is a double. (dist 0 0 1 1) is 1.4142135623730951; compare within a tolerance, or use near?, which does the comparison for you." "A bare top-level NaN auto-prints as NaN while display prints +nan.0.")
  :related ("near?" "in-rect?" "overlap?" "math/hypot" "sqrt")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js, Math.hypot). Not in R7RS-small. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")

(verb :name "atan2" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(atan2 y x) -> number"
  :summary "SHADOW SPELLING of the two-argument R7RS-small `atan`. The angle in radians from the positive x-axis to the point (x, y), in -pi..pi, correct in all four quadrants. Prefer `(atan y x)`."
  :explanation "R7RS-small 6.2.6 already defines the two-argument form: (atan y x) is the quadrant-correct angle. Motoi implements both, over the same Math.atan2, and they agree exactly -- (list (atan 1 1) (atan2 1 1)) is (0.7853981633974483 0.7853981633974483). atan2 is therefore a divergent-name binding carried over from C and JavaScript, not a verb the language needs. The ARGUMENT ORDER is y first, x second, which is the mathematical convention and the reverse of how a point is usually written; getting it backwards gives a reflected angle rather than an error. The whole point of the two-argument form is quadrant information: one-argument (atan 1) cannot tell (1,1) from (-1,-1), while (atan2 -1 -1) is -2.356194490192345 and (atan2 1 1) is 0.7853981633974483."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(atan2 1 1)" :note "0.7853981633974483 — 45 degrees in radians, the angle to the point (1, 1).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (atan 1 1) (atan2 1 1))" :note "(0.7853981633974483 0.7853981633974483) — the same number twice. `atan` with two arguments is the R7RS-small spelling; reach for it.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (atan2 0 1) (atan2 1 0) (atan2 0 -1) (atan2 -1 -1))" :note "(0.0 1.5707963267948966 3.141592653589793 -2.356194490192345) — east, north, west, and the third quadrant as a NEGATIVE angle. The range is -pi..pi, not 0..2pi, so add 2pi yourself if you need a positive bearing.")
  )
  :caveats ("It is a divergent spelling of the R7RS-small two-argument `atan`, bound to the same operation. Prefer (atan y x); atan2 is not portable to other Schemes." "Argument order is y THEN x. Swapping them does not raise; it reflects the angle about the 45-degree line." "The range is -pi..pi. Angles in the lower half-plane come back negative; (atan2 -1 -1) is -2.356194490192345." "(atan2 0 0) is 0. The angle to the origin is not defined mathematically; Motoi picks 0 without saying so." "No type check; non-numeric arguments give +nan.0.")
  :related ("atan" "math/atan2" "sin" "cos" "pi" "angle")
  :provenance "Motoi-own alias for R7RS-small 6.2.6 two-argument `atan` (src/base.js, Math.atan2). Divergent-name binding flagged 2026-08-02.")

(verb :name "exp2" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(exp2 x) -> number"
  :summary "2 raised to the power x -- the same number (expt 2 x) gives. Motoi-own convenience; R7RS-small has `exp` (base e) and `expt`, but nothing base-2."
  :explanation "exp2 is Math.pow(2, x), so it agrees with (expt 2 x) in VALUE but not in exactness: (list (exp2 10) (expt 2 10)) is (1024.0 1024). exp2 sits with the transcendental verbs and always answers an inexact number; expt keeps an exact base raised to an exact integer power exact. It exists because doubling curves come up constantly in the growth-curve and memory-size material, and because it reads as the inverse of log2. The exponent may be fractional or negative -- (exp2 0.5) is the square root of 2 and (exp2 -1) is 0.5. Because exp2 answers a double, it runs into the double's two hard edges. Integers are held without loss only up to 2^53: (exp2 53) is 9007199254740992.0, and adding 1 to it changes nothing. (expt 2 53) has neither edge, because it stays exact. Past about 2^1024 the result is +inf.0 rather than an error or a big integer."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(exp2 10)" :note "1024.0 — two to the tenth.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (exp2 0) (exp2 8) (exp2 -1) (exp2 0.5))" :note "(1.0 256.0 0.5 1.4142135623730951) — the exponent may be zero, negative or fractional; a half power is the square root of 2.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (exp2 53) (+ (exp2 53) 1))" :note "(9007199254740992.0 9007199254740992.0) — adding 1 changes nothing. 2^53 is where a double stops being able to count by ones, and Motoi has no integer type to fall back on.")
  )
  :caveats ("Not R7RS-small. Use (expt 2 x) for portable code; the two give identical results." "Integers above 2^53 are approximate: (+ (exp2 53) 1) is (exp2 53). There is no bignum to fall back on." "Overflow gives +inf.0, not an error: (exp2 1024) is +inf.0, and it auto-prints at the top level as Infinity while display shows +inf.0." "No type check; a non-numeric argument gives +nan.0.")
  :related ("expt" "log2" "exp" "math/pow" "math/exp")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js, Math.pow(2, x)). Not in R7RS-small. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")

(verb :name "log2" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(log2 x) -> number"
  :summary "Base-2 logarithm: how many doublings it takes to reach x. Motoi-own; R7RS-small covers this as (log x 2), which agrees on the cases tried here."
  :explanation "log2 is Math.log2 and is the inverse of exp2: (list (exp2 (log2 7)) (log2 (exp2 7))) is (7 7). R7RS-small 6.2.6 defines the two-argument (log z base) as (/ (log z) (log base)), and Motoi implements it, so (log 1024 2) and (log2 1024) both give 10. log2 is nonetheless worth keeping rather than folding away, for the reason its base-10 sibling is: a dedicated intrinsic does not go through a division of two logarithms and so does not accumulate that division's error. The domain is the one place to be careful. log2 of 0 is -inf.0 and log2 of a negative number is +nan.0; neither raises, and a +nan.0 compares false against everything it later meets."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(log2 1024)" :note "10.0 — 1024 is 2 doubled ten times.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (log 1024 2) (log2 1024))" :note "(10.0 10.0) — the R7RS-small two-argument `log` and this verb agree here. (log x 2) is the portable spelling.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(display (list (log2 0) (log2 -1)))\n(newline)" :note "(-inf.0 +nan.0) — neither raises. Zero gives negative infinity and a negative argument gives a NaN, which compares false against everything downstream, so a bad input goes unnoticed until something far away misbehaves.")
  )
  :caveats ("Not R7RS-small. The portable spelling is (log x 2)." "(log2 0) is -inf.0 and (log2 -1) is +nan.0. Neither raises. Guard the domain yourself if x can reach zero or go negative." "A bare top-level (log2 0) auto-prints as -Infinity while display prints -inf.0. The display spelling is the Scheme one; the two printers disagree." "(log2 10) is 3.321928094887362, a double -- do not compare a logarithm against a typed decimal with =." "No type check.")
  :related ("log" "log10" "exp2" "math/log2" "math/log-base")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js, Math.log2). Not in R7RS-small. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")

(verb :name "log10" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(log10 x) -> number"
  :summary "Base-10 logarithm -- the number of digits story. Motoi-own; R7RS-small covers it as (log x 10), but the two do NOT always agree, and log10 is the more accurate of the pair."
  :explanation "log10 is Math.log10. R7RS-small 6.2.6 defines (log z base) as (/ (log z) (log base)), and that division is where accuracy leaks: (log 1000 10) is 2.9999999999999996 while (log10 1000) is 3, and (log 1e21 10) is 20.999999999999996 while (log10 1e21) is 21. That is not a rounding curiosity to shrug at -- code that takes the floor of a base-10 logarithm to count digits gets the WRONG DIGIT COUNT from the portable spelling and the right one from log10. When you want a base-10 logarithm in Motoi, this is the verb; reach for (log x 10) only when portability matters more than the last bit. The domain caveats are the same as log2's: zero gives -inf.0 and a negative argument gives +nan.0, silently."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(log10 1000)" :note "3.0 — 1000 is 10 to the third.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (log 1000 10) (log10 1000))" :note "(2.9999999999999996 3.0) — they DISAGREE. The R7RS-small two-argument form divides two natural logarithms and loses the last bit; log10 is the intrinsic and lands on 3. Taking the floor of the first answer to count digits gives 2, not 3.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(display (list (log10 0) (log10 -1)))\n(newline)" :note "(-inf.0 +nan.0) — neither raises. A NaN from a negative argument compares false against everything, so the fault surfaces somewhere else entirely.")
  )
  :caveats ("Not R7RS-small. The portable spelling is (log x 10) -- but see the next caveat before you substitute one for the other." "They are NOT interchangeable. (log 1000 10) is 2.9999999999999996 and (log10 1000) is 3. Digit-counting code that floors the result gets a different answer from each." "(log10 0) is -inf.0 and (log10 -1) is +nan.0. Neither raises." "A bare top-level (log10 0) auto-prints as -Infinity while display prints -inf.0; the two printers disagree on infinities and NaN." "No type check.")
  :related ("log" "log2" "math/log10" "math/log-base" "exp2")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js, Math.log10). Not in R7RS-small. Accuracy divergence from (log x 10) measured 2026-08-02 by execution.")

(verb :name "argmin" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "pure"
  :signature "(argmin lst) -> integer or #f"
  :summary "The INDEX of the smallest element of a list, not the element itself. #f for an empty list -- and also #f for a non-list, which is the same answer to two different questions. Motoi-own."
  :explanation "argmin scans the list with < and returns the position of the first smallest element. The name is the mathematician's arg min, so returning a position rather than a value is correct, but it is the thing people get wrong: (argmin (list 5 2 9)) is 1, not 2. Pair it with list-ref when you wanted the value -- (list-ref (list 5 2 9) (argmin (list 5 2 9))) is 2. Ties go to the earliest index, so (argmin (list 1 1 1)) is 0. The comparison is JavaScript's <, not a Scheme numeric comparison, so a list of strings compares lexicographically and (argmin (list \"b\" \"a\" \"c\")) is 1 rather than raising. Two failures share one answer: an empty list and a non-list both give #f. Since 0 is a legitimate result and is TRUE in Scheme, test the result with a predicate before using it, not with if alone -- an index of 0 and a failure of #f are distinguishable, but only if you look."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(argmin (list 5 2 9))" :note "1 — the INDEX of the smallest element, not the element. The smallest value is 2 and it sits in position 1.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list-ref (list 5 2 9) (argmin (list 5 2 9)))" :note "2 — the idiom for getting the value back. argmin gives the position; list-ref turns it into the element.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (argmin (list 1 1 1)) (argmin (list)) (argmin 5))" :note "(0 #f #f) — ties go to the earliest index; an empty list and a non-list both answer #f, so #f does not tell you which mistake you made. Note the first answer is 0, which is TRUE in Scheme, so `if` alone cannot distinguish the found case from the failed one.")
  )
  :caveats ("It returns an INDEX, not an element. This is the single most common misreading of the verb." "Not R7RS-small and not SRFI-1. argmin is Motoi-own; SRFI-1 has no argmin, and there is no argmax in Motoi either." "#f is returned for BOTH an empty list and a non-list argument. The failure is not diagnosable from the return value." "0 is a valid index and is truthy in Scheme, while the failure value #f is falsy. Do not write (if (argmin xs) ...) expecting it to mean \"non-empty\" and then forget that the found index may also be 0 -- it works, but only by accident of Scheme's truthiness, and the same code in a language where 0 is false would break." "Comparison is JavaScript's <, so strings compare lexicographically instead of raising: (argmin (list \"b\" \"a\" \"c\")) is 1." "Ties resolve to the earliest index; there is no way to ask for the last.")
  :related ("min" "max" "list-ref" "fold" "sort")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js). Not in R7RS-small and not in SRFI-1. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")

(verb :name "exact-rational?" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "predicate"
  :signature "(exact-rational? x) -> boolean"
  :summary "#t when x is an exact rational -- which in Motoi means a rational LITERAL like 1/2, or any finite integer-valued double. NOT an R7RS-small verb: the name comes from R6RS, which Motoi otherwise excludes."
  :explanation "Motoi carries a small exact-rational type for literals written with a slash: 1/2 and 1/3 are Rational objects and stay exact. Everything else is a double. exact-rational? is #t for those literals and for any finite double that happens to be a whole number, and #f for everything else. Two consequences catch people. First, arriving at a rational by DIVIDING does not produce one: (exact-rational? 1/3) is #t but (exact-rational? (/ 1 3)) is #f, because the division produced the double 0.3333333333333333. Second, the predicate disagrees with R7RS-small's `rational?` on the same value: (rational? 0.5) is #t and (exact-rational? 0.5) is #f. Both are defensible readings -- 0.5 is a rational number but is not held exactly -- and the pair is a good way to see the exactness boundary. Note the name's provenance: R7RS-small 6.2.6 has `rational?`, `exact?` and `exact-integer?` but NO exact-rational?; that spelling is R6RS 11.7.4, and CLAUDE.md excludes R6RS. Tag it Motoi-own and do not cite R7RS for it."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(exact-rational? 1/2)" :note "#t — a slash literal is held as an exact rational.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (exact-rational? 1/2) (exact-rational? 0.5) (exact-rational? 3) (exact-rational? 3.0))" :note "(#t #f #t #f) — 1/2 and 0.5 are the same number and get opposite answers, because only the first is held exactly. And 3.0 is #f: an integer-valued double is still inexact, and this verb asks for exactness.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (exact-rational? 1/3) (exact-rational? (/ 1 3)))" :note "(#t #t) — the literal and the division agree. (/ 1 3) is the exact rational 1/3, not the double 0.3333333333333333: dividing one exact number by another stays exact.")
  )
  :caveats ("NOT R7RS-small. The name is R6RS 11.7.4, and CLAUDE.md excludes R6RS. It is tagged Motoi-own here on purpose; do not re-tag it R7RS." "It disagrees with R7RS-small `rational?`: (rational? 0.5) is #t while (exact-rational? 0.5) is #f. Neither is broken; they ask different questions." "(exact-rational? 3.0) is #f, because 3.0 is inexact -- the same reason (exact-integer? 3.0) is #f. Both answered #t until the exactness tower landed 2026-08-03." "Computing a rational preserves exactness: (/ 1 3) is the exact 1/3 and answers #t. Literals and divisions agree; before the exactness tower landed 2026-08-03 only slash LITERALS survived." "(exact-rational? +inf.0) is #f, and so is any non-number, so a #f tells you nothing about which of those it was.")
  :related ("rational?" "exact?" "inexact?" "exact-integer?" "numerator" "denominator")
  :provenance "R6RS 11.7.4 name; Motoi-own implementation (lib/base/r7rs-small.js). NOT in R7RS-small -- checked against spec/r7rs-small.slatl, which does not mention it. Std tag corrected to Motoi 2026-08-02.")

(verb :name "=?" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "predicate"
  :signature "(=? a b) -> boolean"
  :summary "SHADOW SPELLING of `equal?`, NOT of `=`. It is deep structural equality bound to the very same function, and it silently ignores every argument past the second. Prefer `equal?` for structures and `=` for numbers."
  :explanation "src/base.js binds =? and equal? to one function, so they cannot disagree: both are R7RS-small's recursive equality over pairs, vectors, strings and bytevectors. The name is the problem. It LOOKS like a predicate spelling of numeric =, and it is not: (=? (list 1 2) (list 1 2)) is #t, which numeric = could never answer, and the arity behaviour differs too. R7RS-small's = is a chain over any number of arguments and correctly answers (= 1 1 2) as #f. =? takes two and drops the rest, so (=? 1 1 2) is #t -- a wrong answer to the question the shape of the call is asking. If you want numbers compared, write =. If you want structures compared, write equal?. There is no case where =? is the right choice, and the conformance ladder says a divergent name over a standard operation is a bug to conform, not a verb to teach."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(=? \"ab\" \"ab\")" :note "#t — two strings with the same characters. This is equal?, not numeric equality.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (=? (list 1 2) (list 1 2)) (equal? (list 1 2) (list 1 2)) (eq? (list 1 2) (list 1 2)))" :note "(#t #t #f) — =? and equal? are literally the same function, and both look inside the lists. eq? does not, and answers #f for two separately built lists.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (=? 1 1 2) (= 1 1 2))" :note "(#f #f) — =? silently drops the third argument and answers #t. The R7RS-small chain comparison = looks at all three and correctly answers #f. Anyone who read =? as a spelling of = gets the wrong answer with no warning.")
  )
  :caveats ("It is NOT numeric =. It is a second name for `equal?`, bound to the same function. The spelling is actively misleading." "It ignores arguments past the second: (=? 1 1 2) is #t while (= 1 1 2) is #f. Nothing raises." "Not R7RS-small and not any SRFI. Use `=` for numbers and `equal?` for structures; there is no situation where =? is the correct choice." "It is filed in this chapter because that is where the undocumented bindings were collected, but it is an equality verb, not a numeric one -- the equality family lives in 16-compare and 52-predicate." "Because it is equal? and not =, (=? 1 1.0) is #f: equal? compares numbers with eqv?, and eqv? requires the same exactness as well as the same value. (= 1 1.0) is #t. That disagreement is the clearest reason never to reach for =? on numbers.")
  :related ("equal?" "=" "eqv?" "eq?" "compare")
  :provenance "Motoi-own alias for R7RS-small 6.1 `equal?` (src/base.js, same function object). Divergent-name binding flagged 2026-08-02; note that the name suggests `=` and the behaviour is `equal?`.")

(verb :name "rng-uniform" :std "Motoi" :library "math" :tier "core" :kind "impure"
  :signature "(rng-uniform) -> number"
  :summary "A random double in [0, 1). Takes no arguments -- any you pass are ignored. Deliberately NOT named `random`, so that every draw is visibly seeded. Motoi-own; R7RS-small has no randomness at all."
  :explanation "rng-uniform is the base draw the whole random surface is built on. It goes through the same seeded generator as random-int, random-range, random-pick and randint, which is the entire reason it is not called `random`: one (with-seed n thunk) governs every one of them, so a cart replays identically no matter which verb it reached for. That property was broken three separate times by verbs that called the host's own random directly, which is why the naming is deliberate rather than decorative. The result is in [0, 1) -- zero is possible, one is not. Seeding makes it reproducible: (with-seed 42 (lambda () (rng-uniform))) is 0.2523451747838408 every run, and successive draws inside one seeded extent advance as you would expect. R7RS-small specifies no random number generator; SRFI-27 is the standard candidate and Motoi does not implement its interface, so this stays Motoi-own."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(with-seed 42 (lambda () (rng-uniform)))" :note "0.2523451747838408 — seeded, so this exact number comes back every run. Without with-seed the value differs each time.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(with-seed 42 (lambda () (list (rng-uniform) (rng-uniform))))" :note "(0.2523451747838408 0.08812504541128874) — two draws inside one seeded extent. The first matches the single-draw example above, which is what makes a seeded cart replayable.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(list (>= (rng-uniform) 0) (< (rng-uniform) 1))" :note "(#t #t) — the range is half-open: 0 can come up, 1 never can. Scaling with (* n (rng-uniform)) therefore never reaches n, which is why random-int can use it for an index without an off-by-one.")
  )
  :caveats ("Not R7RS-small -- the standard has no randomness. It is also not SRFI-27: Motoi does not implement that interface, so this is Motoi-own." "It takes NO arguments and does not check. (rng-uniform 10) ignores the 10 and still returns a number in [0, 1). If you want a bound, use random-int, random-range or randint." "1 is never returned. The interval is [0, 1)." "It is impure. Two calls in a row give different numbers; wrap in (with-seed n thunk) when you need a replay." "It is bound twice, in src/base.js and lib/game/misc.js, over two different seeded-uniform helpers. Both respect with-seed, but do not assume the two files can be edited independently.")
  :related ("with-seed" "random-int" "random-range" "randint" "random-pick")
  :provenance "Motoi-own (src/base.js and lib/game/misc.js, both routed through the seeded generator). Not in R7RS-small; SRFI-27 interface not implemented. Documented 2026-08-02 after execution.")
